The two agree after the 7-char indent, which is what the guard requires.

The pin move 30 -> 31, judged on its own:
  - `seen` counts LINES of scanned constants, not constants. ci.ts's USAGE is
    a single-line constant, so +1 is exactly right, and `seen === 31` is
    clean at f858df0 (confirmed inside `npm test`, not only standalone).
  - It moved in the SAME commit (2ceb00b) as the banner line it accounts for,
    which is the guard's own stated contract; the commit message names the
    reason. `git show --stat 2ceb00b` touches usage.ts and usage.test.ts only.
  - `ci` really is a new 31st entry, not a relocation: packages/commands/src/ci.ts
    does not exist on master.

Four mutations on the guard, each alone:
  U1  drop `[--allow-dirty]` from the BANNER line in usage.ts
        FAIL  packages/commands/src/ci.ts:USAGE line in USAGE
  U2  drop `[--allow-dirty]` from ci.ts's own constant
        FAIL  (same assertion, reporting the drifted literal)
  U3  delete the `bisellium ci` banner line entirely
        FAIL  (same)
  U4  replace ci.ts's USAGE with a non-usage string  (i.e. lose the constant)
        FAIL  scanned every known usage constant   30

So drift in either direction fails, and the pin itself is load-bearing: it is
the only thing that notices the constant dropping out of coverage. This is the
scenario the guard was built for and it behaved as designed.

